artifact.self_contained_problem:
Design a methodological framework that robustly connects integrated observations of massive stars in high-redshift galaxies—where spatial resolution is limited and only restricted wavelength regimes are accessible—to the underlying stellar-physical processes (e.g. mass loss, rotation, binarity, ionizing-photon production, chemical yields) by means of a hierarchical "ladder" of local analogs and testbed environments. Concretely, specify how to combine and cross-calibrate the following rungs so that detailed stellar physics and population-synthesis constraints derived locally can be transferred to integrated high-redshift measurements:

(i) nearby low-metallicity dwarf galaxies treated as integrated-light analogs of high-redshift systems;
(ii) unresolved young massive clusters in the Local Volume, used to constrain population-synthesis models at the cluster scale;
(iii) resolved young massive clusters in which individual stars can be characterized spectroscopically while the cluster environment supplies additional empirical constraints (age, mass function, multiplicity statistics) that anchor population-synthesis testbeds;
(iv) individual stellar systems at very low metallicity, such as iron-poor O-type stars in the Magellanic Bridge, providing direct stellar-physics calibrators.

The problem is to define quantitative procedures—sample selection criteria, observable diagnostics, model ingredients, and statistical inference techniques—that propagate physical constraints from rungs (iv) → (iii) → (ii) → (i) → high-redshift integrated spectra in a controlled and falsifiable way, and to identify which observables and population-synthesis assumptions dominate the systematic uncertainty in this propagation.

